"[2603.07496v1] From Thinker to Society: Security in Hierarchical Autonomy Evolution of AI Agents (https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.07496v1)\nciteturn32196view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.07496v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Cryptography and Security\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 8 Mar 2026 (this version), latest version 21 Mar 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:From Thinker to Society: Security in Hierarchical Autonomy Evolution of AI Agents\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Xiaolei Zhang , cite7†Lu Zhou , cite8†Xiaogang Xu , cite9†Jiafei Wu , cite10†Tianyu Du , cite11†Heqing Huang , cite12†Hao Peng , cite13†Zhe Liu L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled From Thinker to Society: Security in Hierarchical Autonomy Evolution of AI Agents, by Xiaolei Zhang and 7 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite14†View PDF cite15†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Artificial Intelligence (AI) agents have evolved from passive predictive tools into active entities capable of autonomous decision-making and environmental interaction, driven by the reasoning capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs). However, this evolution has introduced critical security vulnerabilities that existing frameworks fail to address.\nL17: The Hierarchical Autonomy Evolution (HAE) framework organizes agent security into three tiers: Cognitive Autonomy (L1) targets internal reasoning integrity; Execution Autonomy (L2) covers tool-mediated environmental interaction; Collective Autonomy (L3) addresses systemic risks in multi-agent ecosystems. We present a taxonomy of threats spanning cognitive manipulation, physical environment disruption, and multi-agent systemic failures, and evaluate existing defenses while identifying key research gaps.\nL18: The findings aim to guide the development of multilayered, autonomy-aware defense architectures for trustworthy AI agent systems.\nL19: Subjects:  | Cryptography and Security (cs.CR); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite16†arXiv:2603.07496 [cs.CR]\nL21:    | (or cite17†arXiv:2603.07496v1 [cs.CR] for this version)\nL22:    | cite18†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2603.07496†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Xiaogang Xu Dr. [cite19†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Sun, 8 Mar 2026 06:45:28 UTC (3,760 KB)\nL27: cite5†[v2] Sat, 21 Mar 2026 07:49:59 UTC (3,760 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled From Thinker to Society: Security in Hierarchical Autonomy Evolution of AI Agents, by Xiaolei Zhang and 7 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite14†View PDF L36:   * cite15†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite20†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite21†view license L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.CR\nL43: \nL44: cite22†< prev |   cite23†next > L45: \nL46: cite24†new | cite25†recent | cite26†2026-03 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite27†cs L51: cite28†cs.AI L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite29†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite30†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite31†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL75: \nL76: Bibliographic Explorer (cite32†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org